    fact: "Slime Molds Solve Complex Mazes Without Having Any Brains At All",
    shortDescription: "Single-celled slime molds successfully navigate intricate labyrinths to locate food sources using only chemical signaling and rhythmic pulses.",
    longDescription: "Physarum polycephalum, a single-celled organism known as a slime mold, exhibits intelligence that defies conventional biological logic. Despite lacking neurons, synapses, or a centralized brain, this golden, amoeba-like organism can navigate complex mazes to find the shortest path to oat flakes. It functions as a decentralized network, utilizing rhythmic contractions to distribute nutrients and information across its entire body simultaneously. When placed in a grid mirroring the layout of Tokyo's railway system, the slime mold spontaneously reconstructed an efficient network nearly identical to the human-designed transit lines. This remarkable behavior challenges our understanding of cognition, proving that complex decision-making and efficient problem-solving do not strictly require a nervous system to function effectively.",
