    fact: "Sea otters hold hands while sleeping to prevent drifting apart.",
    shortDescription: "Sea otters tether themselves together with kelp or by grasping paws to maintain group cohesion during deep sleep.",
    longDescription: "In the chilly waters of the Pacific, sea otters exhibit a heartwarming survival behavior known as rafting. To avoid drifting away from their social group or getting swept out to sea by strong ocean currents while they slumber, they often entangle themselves in strands of giant kelp. Even more fascinating is their habit of holding hands with their mates or pups. This physical connection acts as a biological anchor, ensuring the family unit remains intact throughout the night. This social bonding not only provides safety against the tides but also reinforces the strong maternal and communal ties essential for the survival of the species in their rugged, aquatic environment.",
