    shortDescription: "Microbes living miles beneath the seafloor extract energy from volcanic minerals rather than relying on photosynthesis or organic decay.",
    longDescription: "Deep beneath the seafloor, in the crushing darkness of the Earth's crust, a mysterious biological world thrives. Scientists have discovered thriving communities of microbes living in basalt rock layers miles below the ocean floor. Unlike surface life that depends on the sun, these organisms practice lithotrophy, essentially eating the stone itself. They break down iron and sulfur compounds found within volcanic rock to fuel their metabolism. This discovery fundamentally shifts our understanding of life's resilience, suggesting that the interior of our planet is not a sterile void, but a massive, hidden habitat. It raises tantalizing possibilities that similar life forms might exist within the rocky crusts of other moons and planets across our solar system.",
