  Quantix(
    id: "69fad6ad11a2b83193e19e57",
    fact: "Time moves slower for objects traveling at very high speeds.",
    shortDescription: "Time dilation occurs because the speed of light remains constant for all observers regardless of their relative motion.",
    longDescription: "According to Einstein’s theory of special relativity, time is not an absolute constant but a relative dimension linked to space. As an object accelerates toward the speed of light, the passage of time for that object slows down relative to an observer at rest. This phenomenon, known as time dilation, is not merely a theoretical prediction; it has been empirically verified using atomic clocks on high-speed jets and orbiting satellites. If you were to travel on a spaceship near the speed of light, you would return to Earth to find that decades had passed for everyone else while only a few years had elapsed for you. This fundamental property of our universe defies our everyday intuition.",
    quantixCategory: QuantixCategory.PHYSICS,
