  Quantix(
    id: "69fad79811a2b83193e19e59",
    fact: "Your brain can create false memories that feel completely real.",
    shortDescription: "The human mind reconstructs experiences every time they are recalled, often injecting fiction into historical events.",
    longDescription: "Memory is not a static video recording stored in a library. Instead, the brain treats memory as a dynamic reconstruction project. Every time you retrieve a past experience, the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex assemble fragments of information, often filling in gaps with logical inferences or suggestions from others. Because the brain uses the same neural pathways for imagining the future and recalling the past, these generated details can become indistinguishable from actual sensory input. This phenomenon, known as the misinformation effect, proves that our personal history is a fluid narrative subject to constant revision, meaning that what we perceive as 'truth' can be subtly rewritten by the very act of thinking about it.",
    quantixCategory: QuantixCategory.BRAIN_MIND,
    createdAt: DateTime.parse("2026-05-06T05:54:32.248Z"),
    quizQuestion: "How does the brain process stored memories?",
    quizOptions: ["Static recording", "Dynamic reconstruction", "Video playback", "Constant deletion"],
    quizAnswer: "Dynamic reconstruction",
