  Quantix(
    id: "69fad87b11a2b83193e19e5b",
    fact: "The first computer programmer in history was a Victorian woman.",
    shortDescription: "Ada Lovelace wrote the world's first machine algorithm in 1843 for Charles Babbage's proposed mechanical general-purpose computer, the Analytical Engine.",
    longDescription: "In 1843, Ada Lovelace, the daughter of the poet Lord Byron, published a set of notes on the Analytical Engine, a theoretical machine designed by Charles Babbage. While others saw the device merely as a calculator for numbers, Lovelace realized its true potential went far beyond simple arithmetic. She authored a sophisticated algorithm intended for the machine to calculate Bernoulli numbers, effectively creating the world’s first computer program. Lovelace envisioned that computers could manipulate symbols and potentially compose music or create graphics, anticipating the digital age by over a century. Her profound insight into the logical capabilities of machines laid the fundamental groundwork for modern computer science, forever changing how we perceive human-machine collaboration.",
    quantixCategory: QuantixCategory.SCIENCE_HISTORY,
    createdAt: DateTime.parse("2026-05-06T05:58:19.675Z"),
    quizQuestion: "Who wrote the first computer program?",
    quizOptions: ["Ada Lovelace", "Charles Babbage", "Alan Turing", "Grace Hopper"],
    quizAnswer: "Ada Lovelace",
