    shortDescription: "Soft robotic grippers use pneumatic inflation to mimic human fingers, allowing them to grasp fragile items without any damage.",
    longDescription: "Traditional industrial robots rely on rigid metal pincers that often crush fragile goods like fruit or delicate glassware. To solve this, engineers developed soft robotics made from flexible silicone elastomers. These grippers feature internal air chambers that expand when pressurized, causing the robotic fingers to curl gently around an object. By precisely controlling the air pressure, these robots can mimic the dexterity and sensitivity of a human hand. This technology is revolutionizing logistics and food processing, enabling automated systems to sort ripe strawberries or handle electronics without leaving a single scratch. It represents a fundamental shift from brute-force machinery to biomimetic systems designed to interact safely within human environments and delicate industrial workflows.",
