    shortDescription: "Male seahorses possess a specialized brood pouch where they incubate eggs and deliver fully developed baby seahorses.",
    longDescription: "In the underwater world of Syngnathidae, the traditional roles of reproduction are dramatically flipped. During mating, the female deposits her eggs directly into the male’s ventral brood pouch. Once inside, the male fertilizes the eggs and provides a highly controlled environment, regulating salinity and nutrients as they develop. Over the course of several weeks, the male nurtures these embryos until they are ready to enter the ocean. When the time is right, he undergoes rhythmic muscular contractions to expel dozens of tiny, independent fry into the water. This unique evolutionary adaptation allows the species to maximize reproductive efficiency, freeing the female to begin producing a new batch of eggs almost immediately.",
