    shortDescription: "This super-Earth is composed largely of carbon in the form of diamond and graphite due to extreme pressure.",
    longDescription: "Located about 40 light-years away, the exoplanet 55 Cancri e orbits its host star so closely that a single year lasts only 18 hours. This proximity creates surface temperatures exceeding 2,000 degrees Celsius, essentially melting the planet's surface. Scientific analysis suggests the interior is rich in diamond and graphite, as the carbon-to-oxygen ratio is significantly higher than that of our solar system. Unlike the silicate-rich composition of Earth, this world represents a bizarre class of carbon-heavy planets. The intense gravitational pressure and heat rearrange carbon atoms into crystalline lattices, suggesting that a significant portion of the planet's mass exists as massive, exotic diamond layers beneath a volatile, molten surface.",
