  Quantix(
    id: "69faf80ae62a4a9f68629338",
    fact: "Fluorine is the most reactive element in the entire periodic table.",
    shortDescription: "Fluorine atoms aggressively snatch electrons from almost any other substance, making them chemically violent and highly dangerous.",
    longDescription: "Fluorine is the chemical equivalent of an impatient thief. As the most electronegative element, its atoms possess an insatiable hunger for electrons, pulling them away from virtually any other material they encounter. This extreme reactivity means fluorine can set fire to materials that are typically flame-retardant, including asbestos and even water. In industrial applications, scientists must store fluorine in specialized nickel containers because the metal forms a protective fluoride layer that prevents further damage. Handling this element requires immense caution, as its aggressive nature causes it to vaporize organic tissue upon contact. It is a fundamental building block of modern chemistry, yet it remains one of the most volatile substances ever discovered in nature.",
    quantixCategory: QuantixCategory.CHEMISTRY,
    createdAt: DateTime.parse("2026-05-06T08:12:58.862Z"),
    quizQuestion: "Which element is the most reactive?",
    quizOptions: ["Helium", "Gold", "Fluorine", "Iron"],
    quizAnswer: "Fluorine",
